================================================================================
JMdict / JMnedict (Japanese-multilingual dictionary data)
================================================================================
The layer-0 dictionary (the `word_entries` table) is derived from JMdict, the
Japanese-Multilingual Dictionary. JMdict and the related EDICT/JMnedict files
are the property of the Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group
(EDRDG) and are used in conformance with the Group's license.

  - License: Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
             (CC BY-SA 4.0)
  - Owner:   Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group (EDRDG)
  - Info:    https://www.edrdg.org/  ·  https://www.edrdg.org/edrdg/licence.html

The dictionary data is consumed via "jmdict-simplified", a JSON conversion of
JMdict by Stanislav Kalashnik (scriptin), also distributed under CC BY-SA 4.0:

  - https://github.com/scriptin/jmdict-simplified

The JMdict release file is downloaded at seed time and is NOT redistributed in
this repository. Dictionary entries derived from JMdict are a ShareAlike work:
redistribution of that derived data must preserve this attribution and the
CC BY-SA 4.0 terms.

================================================================================
Japanese tokenization (kuromoji.js + IPADIC)
================================================================================
Layer-0 tokenization uses kuromoji.js, which bundles the IPADIC morphological
dictionary. kuromoji.js and IPADIC are obtained as npm dependencies and retain
their own licenses (see their respective packages under node_modules and their
upstream repositories).

================================================================================
Frequency lists
================================================================================
Word-frequency ranking is optional and user-supplied: no third-party frequency
list is bundled in this repository (the committed `frequency-sample.tsv` is a
small synthetic fixture for tests). If you seed a real frequency list, you are
responsible for complying with and attributing that list's own license.

